Japan's big wireless firms may cut prices of Apple Inc.'s (AAPL) cheapest new iPhone -- the XR -- next week, due to subsidies being offered by the company, The Wall Street Journal reported (https://www.wsj.com/articles/not-so-big-in-japan-apple-cuts-price-of-iphone-xr-to-boost-sales-1542896625?tesla=y) Thursday, citing sources. Concerns over demand for Apple's newest phones have weighed on the stock since a poorly received earnings report earlier this month. Foxconn Industrial Internet Co. , Apple's biggest iPhone manufacturer, said earlier in the week that it would slash costs by $2.9 billion, owing to a "difficult and competitive year." A profit and revenue warning from optical equipment supplier Lumentum Holdings Inc. (LITE) , which counts Apple as one of its biggest customers, triggered sharp losses for the iPhone maker last week. Apple shares closed at $176.78 on Wednesday, 23.7% below an Oct. 3 record close of $232.07. Many on Wall Street define a bear market as a decline of 20% or more from a bull-market high.
-Barbara Kollmeyer;
